// Typed channels: a bounded ring buffer or an unbuffered rendezvous between
// green threads, plus `select` for waiting on several at once.
//
// Stage-2 port of `runtime/chan.zig` (#1365). Both exist during the port;
// #1367/#1369 retire the Zig original.
//
// Semantics are the Zig's, which are the spec's (SPEC §16.2-16.3, mirroring
// Go): send on a closed channel is an error, receive on a closed-and-drained
// channel yields `(zero, false)`, a nil channel blocks forever, `close` wakes
// every blocked peer, and `select` picks uniformly at random among the cases
// ready at the moment it runs. Buffer discipline, wait-queue order and the
// select winner-take-all rule are unchanged — a channel that is "better" but
// different would deadlock programs that work today.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THERE IS ONLY ONE CHANNEL TYPE HERE, AND THAT IS THE ABI'S CHOICE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `chan.zig` is generic (`Chan(T)`) and therefore needs `ChanVTable` — nine
// function pointers per instantiation — so that `select` can drive cases whose
// `T` differ. ABI.md §11 then collapses all of it: "every `chan<T>` a Bit
// program declares is realized at the runtime boundary as a channel of one
// 8-byte word (`WordChan = Chan(u64)`) — never a per-`T` instantiation".
//
// So the vtable's entire job is to bridge a genericity the ABI does not have.
// This port is `WordChan` and calls its own operations directly. That removes
// the indirection rather than reimplementing it, which matters twice over:
// `@nosplit` REJECTS A CALL THROUGH A FUNCTION VALUE (E0075), so a vtable is
// not merely unnecessary here but unexpressible — and `SelectNode`'s reason for
// existing (a layout-compatible stand-in for a `Waiter` whose `T` select cannot
// name) goes with it, along with the `comptime` assert that policed it. One
// waiter shape, no cast, nothing to keep in sync.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY EVERY FUNCTION HERE IS `@nosplit`, AND WHAT THAT COSTS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Task bodies must be `@nosplit` today (see `schedWorkerRun`'s comment: a
// safepoint snapshots registers into one process-wide frame and collects
// unsynchronized, so no worker loop may reach one — #1429). A `@nosplit` body
// may only call `@nosplit` functions, and channel operations are called FROM
// task bodies, so this module inherits the attribute wholesale.
//
// Three consequences, none of them chosen:
//
//   1. NO PANIC. `chan.zig` panics on "send on closed channel" and "close of
//      closed channel"; `panic` is not reachable from a `@nosplit` body. Both
//      are reported as a `false` return instead, and the ABI wrapper that
//      raises them as panics is #1367's half. This is the same trade
//      `schedUnpark` already made, for the same reason.
//   2. NO CALLBACKS. `scanRegistryRoots(mark)` and `forEachBuffered(visit)`
//      take function pointers. Both are re-exposed as cursors the collector
//      drives (`chanRegHead`/`chanRegNext`, `chanBufferedAt`) — exactly what
//      `runtime/sched` did to `forEachOtherStack` for the same reason.
//   3. NO CLOCK. `selectRandom` seeds from `monoNs()`; a clock is a syscall or
//      an extern call. See `selectSeed` for what replaces it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PARK RACE, WHICH IS THE ONE REAL DESIGN CHANGE IN THIS FILE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `chan.zig` links a waiter onto a channel's queue from inside `park`'s `setup`
// callback — "never before, and never after". That timing is the whole
// correctness argument: `setup` runs strictly after the task's context is saved
// and its state is visibly `.parked`, so an unparker can never see a waiter
// belonging to a task that is not yet parkable.
//
// THAT HOOK DOES NOT EXIST HERE. `schedPark` has no `ParkFn`, because passing
// one is a call through a function value, which `@nosplit` rejects (E0075).
// `runtime/sched`'s own comment names this as the seam "#1365 will need closed
// properly", so it is the inherited problem rather than a new one.
//
// This port closes it from the other end. A waiter is registered BEFORE parking,
// under the channel lock — which makes the waiter visible early rather than
// late, so the lost-wakeup risk moves from "a waker cannot find the task" to "a
// waker finds the task before it has finished parking". The second is
// detectable and the first is not: `schedUnpark` compare-and-exchanges
// `taskParked -> taskRunnable` and RETURNS FALSE when the task was not parked,
// so a waker can see the case exactly. `chanWake` therefore waits for the
// parking task to arrive before unparking it.
//
// WHY THAT WAIT TERMINATES, which is the only thing that makes this sound:
// a task appears on a wait queue only while it is RUNNING (it pushed itself
// there), and green threads here are not preemptible, so it holds its worker's
// CPU continuously from the push to the context switch — a lock release and a
// call. `taskParked` is stored by that worker immediately after the switch
// saves the context. So the waited-for transition is already in flight on
// another OS thread, with no lock in between that the waker could be holding.
//
// This is the SAME liveness assumption `runtime/spinlock` already makes of
// every critical section in the runtime — that a thread holding a word another
// thread spins on continues to be scheduled — not a new class of hazard. It is
// bounded anyway (`WAKE_MAX_SPINS`) so that a wedged scheduler degrades into a
// dropped wakeup rather than a hung process, and it spins on the same
// architectural hint instructions `runtime/spinlock` and the scheduler's idle
// backoff use.
//
// A WELCOME CONSEQUENCE: registering early removes the need for `setup`'s
// recheck. `chan.zig` must re-test the channel condition inside the callback
// because the gap between its own failed poll and registration is exactly when
// another thread could have made the case ready. Registering under the same
// lock acquisition that observed "not ready" closes that gap by construction,
// so there is no window to recheck. The behaviour is identical; one of the two
// mechanisms that produced it is simply no longer needed.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MEMORY: CALLER-OWNED BLOCKS, AND A PER-TASK SCRATCH AREA
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A channel, a ring buffer and a select descriptor array are word-indexed
// blocks the caller supplies, exactly as `runtime/sched`, `runtime/alloc` and
// `runtime/gc` take theirs. Nothing here allocates.
//
// A waiter is different: `chan.zig` puts it on the blocking function's own
// stack frame (`var w: Waiter`), which this port cannot do — `ptrOf` on a local
// is rejected inside a `@nosplit` body (#1421), so a local's address cannot be
// handed to a queue. And the ABI's entry points (`bit_rt_chan_send(ch, value)`)
// have no spare parameter for scratch, so the caller cannot supply it either.
//
// So a waiter lives at the base of the parking task's OWN STACK, in the words
// immediately above the self-pointer and tag `schedTaskInit` writes there. That
// is the same "the running task's stack is the one piece of per-thread state
// that needs no help from the operating system" trick `schedCurrentTask` rests
// on, reused rather than reinvented. It has the lifetime a waiter needs exactly
// — the scratch dies with the stack it belongs to, which is precisely when the
// task that could be waiting on it is gone — and it costs no allocation.
//
// The area is the LOWEST `chanScratchWords` words of the stack, and a stack
// grows down from its top, so the scratch is the last memory a deepening call
// chain would reach. A task that overran it has already overflowed its stack.
//
// 264 words = 2112 bytes out of a 64KiB stack: the eight fixed words below,
// `chanMaxSelect` waiter nodes, and two `chanMaxSelect`-entry index arrays.
export const chanScratchWords: int = 264

import { spinAcquire, spinRelease, spinCellAt } from "../"
import { taskAt, taskState, taskParked, taskStackBase, schedUnpark, schedPark } from "../sched"
import { schedCurrentTask } from "../sched"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Channel block
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `chan.zig`'s `Chan(T)` fields, in declaration order, as a word-indexed block:
//
//   0   lock        an i32 cell, addressed via `spinCellAt`
//   1   closed
//   2   buf         base address of the caller-owned ring storage (0 when cap 0)
//   3   cap
//   4   head
//   5   count
//   6   sendq head        the two wait queues, each a singly-linked FIFO of
//   7   sendq tail        waiter addresses under this channel's lock
//   8   recvq head
//   9   recvq tail
//   10  isRef       whether a buffered word is itself a GC reference (ABI §11)
//   11  regNext     intrusive link for the root-scanning registry
//   12  sched       the scheduler this channel's wakeups go through
//
// `sched` is a field here where `chan.zig` threads `sp: *Scheduler` through
// every call. Forced by the ABI: `bit_rt_chan_send(ch, value)` has no scheduler
// parameter and its arity is fixed, so a channel must be able to reach one on
// its own. Equivalent — there is one process-wide scheduler (ABI.md §9) — and
// it also makes `chanClose` callable from a non-worker thread, which the Zig's
// signature already allowed and this file's tests rely on.
export const chanWords: int = 13

const chLock: int = 0
const chClosed: int = 1
const chBuf: int = 2
const chCap: int = 3
const chHead: int = 4
const chCount: int = 5
const chSendHead: int = 6
const chSendTail: int = 7
const chRecvHead: int = 8
const chRecvTail: int = 9
const chIsRef: int = 10
const chRegNext: int = 11
const chSched: int = 12

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Waiter block
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `chan.zig`'s `Waiter`, field for field and in the same order. It kept that
// order to stay layout-compatible with `SelectNode`; here there is only one
// shape, so the order is preserved simply because there is no reason to disturb
// a working structure.
//
//   0  task      the parked task's address
//   1  elem      address of the value slot (in for a send, out for a receive)
//   2  ok        address of the outcome slot
//   3  select    address of the select-shared block, 0 for a plain waiter
//   4  caseIdx   which select case this registration is
//   5  next      queue link
const wTask: int = 0
const wElem: int = 1
const wOk: int = 2
const wSelect: int = 3
const wCase: int = 4
const wNext: int = 5
const waiterWords: int = 6

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Per-task scratch, at the base of the task's own stack
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   0        task self-pointer      written by `schedTaskInit`
//   1        stack tag              written by `schedTaskInit`
//   2        select seed counter
//   3        select shared: done    the winner-take-all CAS word
//   4        select shared: winner
//   5        elem                   plain send/recv value slot
//   6        ok                     plain send/recv outcome slot
//   7        reserved
//   8..199   waiter nodes           `chanMaxSelect` * `waiterWords`
const scrCounter: int = 2
const scrSelDone: int = 3
const scrSelWinner: int = 4
const scrElem: int = 5
const scrOk: int = 6
const scrNodes: int = 8

// The two index arrays select needs, past the waiter nodes: `scrOrder` holds
// the case indices sorted by channel address (the lock order) and `scrShuffled`
// holds them in the random order the poll runs in. They are separate because the
// two orders are answers to different questions — see `chanSelect`.
const scrOrder: int = 200
const scrShuffled: int = 232

// `chan.zig`'s `max_select_cases`, unchanged: select's registrations are
// fixed-size and preallocated (Power of 10 — no unbounded resource after
// startup). Nobody writes a 32-way select; the constant is a bound, not a
// budget.
export const chanMaxSelect: int = 32

// Select case direction, matching ABI.md §11's `SelectCaseDesc.dir` encoding
// (0 recv / 1 send) — codegen writes these, so they are ABI and not free.
export const chanDirRecv: int = 0
export const chanDirSend: int = 1

// `SelectCaseDesc { dir, chan, word, ok }`, ABI.md §11's 32-byte descriptor,
// as word indices. `word` is IN/OUT: a send case's value on the way in, a recv
// case's received value on the way out.
export const selCaseWords: int = 4
const scDir: int = 0
const scChan: int = 1
const scWord: int = 2
const scOk: int = 3

// The scratch area of the task at `task`.
@nosplit function scratchOf(task: int): *i64 {
  let base = *(taskAt(task) + taskStackBase)
  return taskAt(base)
}

// Waiter node `i` of a task's scratch area.
@nosplit function nodeAt(scr: *i64, i: int): *i64 {
  return scr + scrNodes + i * waiterWords
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Wait queues
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `chan.zig`'s `WaitQueue(W)`: a singly-linked FIFO of waiter addresses guarded
// by the owning channel's lock. Two of them per channel, so each operation
// takes the head/tail slot pair to work on rather than a queue object — the
// same "a structure is a block plus an offset" shape the rest of the port uses.
//
// FIFO is the discipline, not an accident: it is what makes a channel fair
// between senders blocked on a full buffer.

@nosplit function wqPush(ch: *i64, headSlot: int, w: *i64) {
  *(w + wNext) = 0
  let tail = *(ch + headSlot + 1)
  if (tail == 0) {
    *(ch + headSlot) = int(w)
  } else {
    *(taskAt(tail) + wNext) = int(w)
  }
  *(ch + headSlot + 1) = int(w)
}

@nosplit function wqPop(ch: *i64, headSlot: int): int {
  let head = *(ch + headSlot)
  if (head == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let next = *(taskAt(head) + wNext)
  *(ch + headSlot) = next
  if (next == 0) {
    *(ch + headSlot + 1) = 0
  }
  *(taskAt(head) + wNext) = 0
  return head
}

// Unlink a specific waiter. Only ever used to cancel a losing select
// registration, and a no-op when the target is absent — either a completer
// already popped it, or it was never registered because the winner resolved
// first. `chan.zig`'s `WaitQueue.remove`, including that tolerance.
//
// The walk is bounded by the queue's own length, which is bounded by the number
// of live tasks; `chanMaxWaiters` is the runaway backstop, the same shape
// `schedRegCount` uses on the task registry.
const chanMaxWaiters: int = 16777216

@nosplit function wqRemove(ch: *i64, headSlot: int, target: int) {
  let prev = 0
  let cur = *(ch + headSlot)
  let n = 0
  while (cur != 0 && n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let next = *(taskAt(cur) + wNext)
    if (cur == target) {
      if (prev == 0) {
        *(ch + headSlot) = next
      } else {
        *(taskAt(prev) + wNext) = next
      }
      if (*(ch + headSlot + 1) == cur) {
        *(ch + headSlot + 1) = prev
      }
      *(taskAt(cur) + wNext) = 0
      return
    }
    prev = cur
    cur = next
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Waking a parked peer
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// See the header for why this waits and why the wait terminates. The bound
// exists so that termination is provable by READING the loop rather than by
// trusting the scheduler, exactly as `schedWorkerRun`'s trip bound is.
//
// Exhausting it drops a wakeup, which is a hang for the peer — but the only way
// to reach it is a scheduler that has already stopped running the parking task,
// in which case the peer was never going to be resumed anyway. Spinning forever
// would convert that into a hung waker as well.
const WAKE_MAX_SPINS: int = 1000000000

@nosplit function chanWake(s: *i64, task: int) {
  let t = taskAt(task)
  let n = 0
  while (n < WAKE_MAX_SPINS) {
    if (atomicLoad(t + taskState) == taskParked) {
      schedUnpark(s, task)
      return
    }
    asm {
      arm64 { 0xD503203F }        // yield
      x64   { 0xF3, 0x90 }        // pause
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// True if this waiter may be completed by the caller.
//
// `chan.zig`'s `claim`, unchanged in mechanism. A plain waiter always succeeds:
// it sits on exactly one queue and nothing else contends for it. A select
// registration claims by CAS on its shared `done` word, so exactly one of the
// several channels a select is parked on can complete it; every other channel's
// attempt loses the CAS and must treat the entry as if it had never been queued.
// This is Go's `sudog.isSelect`/`selectDone` design, which is where `chan.zig`
// took it from.
@nosplit function claim(w: *i64): bool {
  let sel = *(w + wSelect)
  if (sel == 0) {
    return true
  }
  return atomicCmpxchg(taskAt(sel), 0, 1)
}

// Record the winning case (select only) and wake the waiter's task.
@nosplit function finish(s: *i64, w: *i64) {
  let sel = *(w + wSelect)
  if (sel != 0) {
    *(taskAt(sel) + 1) = *(w + wCase)
  }
  chanWake(s, *(w + wTask))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ring buffer — cap > 0 only
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@nosplit function pushBuf(ch: *i64, value: int) {
  let buf = taskAt(*(ch + chBuf))
  let capacity = *(ch + chCap)
  let count = *(ch + chCount)
  *(buf + (*(ch + chHead) + count) % capacity) = value
  *(ch + chCount) = count + 1
}

@nosplit function popBuf(ch: *i64): int {
  let buf = taskAt(*(ch + chBuf))
  let capacity = *(ch + chCap)
  let head = *(ch + chHead)
  let value = *(buf + head)
  *(ch + chHead) = (head + 1) % capacity
  *(ch + chCount) = *(ch + chCount) - 1
  return value
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Direct handoffs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// These skip the buffer entirely, and are also how an unbuffered channel
// (cap 0) always moves a value. `chan.zig`'s `tryHandoffToReceiver` /
// `tryHandoffFromSender` / `promoteQueuedSender`, unchanged: each pops until it
// finds a waiter it can claim, because an unclaimable one belongs to a select
// that another channel already won and must be discarded rather than retried.

@nosplit function tryHandoffToReceiver(ch: *i64, value: int, s: *i64): bool {
  let n = 0
  while (n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chRecvHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      return false
    }
    let w = taskAt(addr)
    if (claim(w)) {
      *(taskAt(*(w + wElem))) = value
      *(taskAt(*(w + wOk))) = 1
      finish(s, w)
      return true
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
  return false
}

@nosplit function tryHandoffFromSender(ch: *i64, valueOut: *i64, s: *i64): bool {
  let n = 0
  while (n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chSendHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      return false
    }
    let w = taskAt(addr)
    if (claim(w)) {
      *valueOut = *(taskAt(*(w + wElem)))
      *(taskAt(*(w + wOk))) = 1
      finish(s, w)
      return true
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
  return false
}

// After a value leaves the buffer, pull one blocked sender into the freed slot.
// Only possible when the buffer had been full, and it is what preserves FIFO
// order across buffered sends — without it a sender that blocked on a full
// buffer would be overtaken by every later sender that found room.
@nosplit function promoteQueuedSender(ch: *i64, s: *i64) {
  let n = 0
  while (n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chSendHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      return
    }
    let w = taskAt(addr)
    if (claim(w)) {
      pushBuf(ch, *(taskAt(*(w + wElem))))
      *(taskAt(*(w + wOk))) = 1
      finish(s, w)
      return
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Non-blocking attempts — the channel lock must be held
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `chan.zig`'s `attemptSend`/`attemptRecv`, in the same order, because the order
// IS the semantics: a waiting receiver takes precedence over the buffer (so an
// unbuffered channel works at all, and so a rendezvous does not pointlessly
// round-trip through storage), and on the receive side a buffered value takes
// precedence over a waiting sender (so FIFO across the buffer holds).

@nosplit function attemptSend(ch: *i64, value: int, s: *i64): bool {
  if (tryHandoffToReceiver(ch, value, s)) {
    return true
  }
  if (*(ch + chCount) < *(ch + chCap)) {
    pushBuf(ch, value)
    return true
  }
  return false
}

// `attemptRecv` reports through a status rather than an out-parameter `ok`.
//
// `chan.zig` writes `ok` through a pointer because its caller always has a
// stack local to point at. This port's caller does not: a local's address is
// unavailable inside a `@nosplit` body (#1421), so an out-parameter `ok` would
// force every receive — including the non-blocking fast path — to reach for the
// per-task scratch, and that would make `chanRecv` fault when called from a
// thread that is not a task. A status return keeps the fast path task-free,
// which is what lets a non-task thread drain a channel.
const rcvNone: int = 0
const rcvValue: int = 1
const rcvClosed: int = 2

@nosplit function attemptRecv(ch: *i64, valueOut: *i64, s: *i64): int {
  if (*(ch + chCount) > 0) {
    *valueOut = popBuf(ch)
    promoteQueuedSender(ch, s)
    return rcvValue
  }
  if (tryHandoffFromSender(ch, valueOut, s)) {
    return rcvValue
  }
  if (*(ch + chClosed) != 0) {
    // The zero word, and `ok` false to say so. For a reference element that
    // zero IS a null pointer, which is the whole reason the two-result receive
    // exists (ABI.md §11) — a receiver must be able to tell "closed" from "a
    // real value" before dereferencing it.
    *valueOut = 0
    return rcvClosed
  }
  return rcvNone
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Setup
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Prepare a channel over caller-owned storage. `bufAddr` addresses `cap` words
// and is ignored when `cap` is 0 (the unbuffered case, where `count < cap` is
// `0 < 0` and every value moves by direct handoff — `chan.zig` needs no special
// case for it either).
export @nosplit function chanInit(ch: *i64, bufAddr: int, capacity: int, isRef: bool, s: *i64) {
  atomicStore(spinCellAt(ch, chLock), 0)
  *(ch + chClosed) = 0
  *(ch + chBuf) = bufAddr
  *(ch + chCap) = capacity
  *(ch + chHead) = 0
  *(ch + chCount) = 0
  *(ch + chSendHead) = 0
  *(ch + chSendTail) = 0
  *(ch + chRecvHead) = 0
  *(ch + chRecvTail) = 0
  if (isRef) {
    *(ch + chIsRef) = 1
  } else {
    *(ch + chIsRef) = 0
  }
  *(ch + chRegNext) = 0
  *(ch + chSched) = int(s)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Blocking send
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Returns false for "send on a closed channel", which `chan.zig` reports by
// panicking. See the header: `panic` is not reachable from a `@nosplit` body,
// and the ABI wrapper raises it. Both of the Zig's two panic points are checked
// here in the same two places — before attempting, and after being woken by a
// `close` that found this sender queued.
//
// MUST BE CALLED FROM TASK CODE. Off a task `schedCurrentTask` returns 0 and
// the scratch lookup faults, which is `runtime/sched`'s stated contract for
// every function that can park and the honest equivalent of `chan.zig`'s
// `sched.park` on a non-task thread.
export @nosplit function chanSend(ch: *i64, value: int): bool {
  let s = taskAt(*(ch + chSched))
  spinAcquire(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
  if (*(ch + chClosed) != 0) {
    spinRelease(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
    return false
  }
  if (attemptSend(ch, value, s)) {
    spinRelease(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
    return true
  }

  // Blocked. Register under the SAME lock acquisition that just observed the
  // channel unable to take the value — that adjacency is what removes the
  // recheck `chan.zig` needs inside its park callback (see the header).
  let task = schedCurrentTask()
  let scr = scratchOf(task)
  *(scr + scrElem) = value
  *(scr + scrOk) = 0
  let w = nodeAt(scr, 0)
  *(w + wTask) = task
  *(w + wElem) = int(scr + scrElem)
  *(w + wOk) = int(scr + scrOk)
  *(w + wSelect) = 0
  *(w + wCase) = 0
  wqPush(ch, chSendHead, w)

  // Never hold a spinlock across the context switch: the holder stops running,
  // and every spinner is `@nosplit` and so cannot yield.
  spinRelease(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
  schedPark()

  let ok = *(scr + scrOk)
  return ok != 0
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Blocking receive
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Writes the received value through `valueOut` and returns `ok` — false meaning
// the channel was closed and drained, with the zero word written. `chan.zig`
// returns both in a `RecvResult` struct; a two-word return is the shape ABI.md
// §11 gives `bit_rt_chan_recv`, and splitting it into an out-parameter plus a
// bool is how that lands on a `@nosplit` Bit signature without a struct return.
export @nosplit function chanRecv(ch: *i64, valueOut: *i64): bool {
  let s = taskAt(*(ch + chSched))

  spinAcquire(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
  let st = attemptRecv(ch, valueOut, s)
  if (st != rcvNone) {
    spinRelease(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
    return st == rcvValue
  }

  // Only the blocking path needs a task: the value slot moves to the per-task
  // scratch so that the waker has somewhere stable to write it, `valueOut`
  // being a caller frame that is about to be suspended.
  let task = schedCurrentTask()
  let scr = scratchOf(task)
  let w = nodeAt(scr, 0)
  *(w + wTask) = task
  *(w + wElem) = int(scr + scrElem)
  *(w + wOk) = int(scr + scrOk)
  *(w + wSelect) = 0
  *(w + wCase) = 0
  wqPush(ch, chRecvHead, w)
  spinRelease(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
  schedPark()

  *valueOut = *(scr + scrElem)
  let ok = *(scr + scrOk)
  return ok != 0
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Close
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Wakes EVERY blocked peer — receivers with `(zero, false)`, senders with the
// `ok` that makes them report "send on closed channel". Returns false for
// "close of a closed channel", the Zig's second panic.
//
// TEARDOWN IS THE POINT OF THIS FUNCTION, and this project has already paid for
// getting it wrong: a QUIC test hung because teardown never released its
// `read`/`finish`/`acceptStream` waiters, and because `finish` drained on
// packets rather than bytes — a condition a departed peer can never satisfy. So
// both loops here drain their queue to empty rather than waking a bounded batch,
// and neither waits on any condition another party must still supply. After
// this returns, both queues are empty and every task that was on them is
// runnable.
//
// Safe from a non-task thread: it never parks, and it reaches the scheduler
// through the channel's own `sched` field rather than through the caller.
export @nosplit function chanClose(ch: *i64): bool {
  let s = taskAt(*(ch + chSched))
  spinAcquire(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
  if (*(ch + chClosed) != 0) {
    spinRelease(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
    return false
  }
  *(ch + chClosed) = 1

  let n = 0
  while (n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chRecvHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      n = chanMaxWaiters
    } else {
      let w = taskAt(addr)
      if (claim(w)) {
        *(taskAt(*(w + wElem))) = 0
        *(taskAt(*(w + wOk))) = 0
        finish(s, w)
      }
      n = n + 1
    }
  }

  let m = 0
  while (m < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chSendHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      m = chanMaxWaiters
    } else {
      let w = taskAt(addr)
      if (claim(w)) {
        *(taskAt(*(w + wOk))) = 0
        finish(s, w)
      }
      m = m + 1
    }
  }

  spinRelease(spinCellAt(ch, chLock))
  return true
}

// Whether the channel is closed. Not a `chan.zig` export — added because the
// root-scanning cursor and the tests need to observe the flag without taking
// the lock, and a plain load of a word that only ever goes 0 -> 1 is race-free
// in the only direction that matters.
export @nosplit function chanClosed(ch: *i64): bool {
  return *(ch + chClosed) != 0
}

// How many values are currently buffered. `len(ch)` in spec terms.
export @nosplit function chanCount(ch: *i64): int {
  return *(ch + chCount)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Select
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `cases` is `n` contiguous `SelectCaseDesc`s (ABI.md §11). Returns the index of
// the case that fired, or `n` when `hasDefault` and nothing was ready — which is
// the ABI's encoding of "the default clause ran", not an error.
//
// A send case on a closed channel is "ready" and reports its outcome through the
// descriptor's `ok` word, mirroring a plain `chanSend` returning false rather
// than the Zig's panic.

// The select seed.
//
// `chan.zig` seeds xoshiro256 from `monoNs() ^ counter ^ &stack_marker`, and
// says in its own header that fairness needs an UNBIASED shuffle, not an
// unpredictable one. A clock is a syscall or an extern call, so `monoNs` is not
// reachable from a `@nosplit` body — and the process-wide `select_seed_counter`
// would need an atomic on module state, which needs `ptrOf` (#1421).
//
// Both are replaced by state that is per-task and therefore raced by nobody: the
// task's own address, mixed with a counter living in that task's scratch. The
// counter alone makes successive selects by one task differ; the address makes
// two tasks at the same iteration differ. That is exactly the property the
// shuffle needs. The mix is splitmix64's finalizer, the same one `runtime/rand`
// uses for scalar keys.
// The multiplier and increment are Knuth's 64-bit LCG constants, also used by
// `shuffleOrder` below. Written in decimal because splitmix64's usual hex
// constants (0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 and friends) do not fit a signed `int`.
const mixMul: int = 6364136223846793005
const mixInc: int = 1442695040888963407

@nosplit function selectSeed(scr: *i64, task: int): int {
  let c = *(scr + scrCounter) + 1
  *(scr + scrCounter) = c
  let x = task + c * mixMul
  x = (x ^ (x >> 29)) * mixMul + mixInc
  return x ^ (x >> 32)
}

// Fisher-Yates over the case order, so that "the first ready case wins" is a
// uniform-random pick among the ready set. `chan.zig` shuffles the same array
// for the same reason.
@nosplit function shuffleOrder(order: *i64, n: int, seed: int) {
  let state = seed
  let i = n - 1
  while (i > 0) {
    state = state * 6364136223846793005 + 1442695040888963407
    let r = (state >> 33) & 0x7FFFFFFF
    let j = r % (i + 1)
    let a = *(order + i)
    *(order + i) = *(order + j)
    *(order + j) = a
    i = i - 1
  }
}

// The address of case `i`'s channel.
@nosplit function caseChan(cases: *i64, i: int): int {
  return *(cases + i * selCaseWords + scChan)
}

// Sort `order` by channel address — insertion sort, `n <= chanMaxSelect`.
//
// THIS IS THE GLOBAL LOCK ORDER, and it is the only thing stopping two
// concurrent selects that share two channels from deadlocking each other by
// taking them in opposite orders. Sorting also puts duplicate channels adjacent,
// which is what lets `lockAll` skip the second lock of a channel named by two
// cases instead of self-deadlocking on it.
@nosplit function sortByAddr(cases: *i64, order: *i64, n: int) {
  let i = 1
  while (i < n) {
    let j = i
    while (j > 0 && caseChan(cases, *(order + j - 1)) > caseChan(cases, *(order + j))) {
      let a = *(order + j - 1)
      *(order + j - 1) = *(order + j)
      *(order + j) = a
      j = j - 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

@nosplit function lockAll(cases: *i64, order: *i64, n: int) {
  let last = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let a = caseChan(cases, *(order + i))
    if (a != last) {
      spinAcquire(spinCellAt(taskAt(a), chLock))
      last = a
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

@nosplit function unlockAll(cases: *i64, order: *i64, n: int) {
  let last = 0
  let i = n
  while (i > 0) {
    i = i - 1
    let a = caseChan(cases, *(order + i))
    if (a != last) {
      spinRelease(spinCellAt(taskAt(a), chLock))
      last = a
    }
  }
}

// One non-blocking pass with every involved channel already locked. Returns the
// firing case index, or `n` when nothing was ready. `chan.zig`'s `pollOnce`.
@nosplit function pollOnce(cases: *i64, order: *i64, n: int, s: *i64): int {
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let idx = *(order + i)
    let c = cases + idx * selCaseWords
    let ch = taskAt(*(c + scChan))
    if (*(c + scDir) == chanDirRecv) {
      let st = attemptRecv(ch, c + scWord, s)
      if (st != rcvNone) {
        if (st == rcvValue) {
          *(c + scOk) = 1
        } else {
          *(c + scOk) = 0
        }
        return idx
      }
    } else {
      if (*(ch + chClosed) != 0) {
        // Ready, and it will report "send on closed channel" — exactly what a
        // plain send does, rather than being skipped as not-ready.
        *(c + scOk) = 0
        return idx
      }
      if (attemptSend(ch, *(c + scWord), s)) {
        *(c + scOk) = 1
        return idx
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return n
}

// Block until exactly one of `n` cases can proceed, chosen uniformly at random
// among those ready. `hasDefault` makes it a single non-blocking attempt.
//
// `n == 0` is `select {}`, which blocks forever — parking with nothing that can
// ever wake it, matching the Zig.
//
// A nil-channel case is the caller's responsibility to omit, as in Go and as in
// `chan.zig`: such a case is simply never ready and never wins.
export @nosplit function chanSelect(cases: *i64, n: int, hasDefault: bool): int {
  let task = schedCurrentTask()
  let scr = scratchOf(task)

  if (n == 0) {
    schedPark()
    return 0
  }

  // `order` and the shared block both live in this task's scratch, so no case
  // of this select is reachable from any other task's memory.
  let order = scr + scrOrder
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    *(order + i) = i
    i = i + 1
  }
  sortByAddr(cases, order, n)

  let s = taskAt(*(taskAt(caseChan(cases, 0)) + chSched))

  // The poll runs in shuffled order; the LOCKS are taken in address order. The
  // two orders are independent on purpose — fairness is about which ready case
  // is chosen, deadlock-freedom is about the order the locks are taken in, and
  // conflating them would sacrifice one for the other.
  let shuffled = scr + scrShuffled
  let k = 0
  while (k < n) {
    *(shuffled + k) = k
    k = k + 1
  }
  shuffleOrder(shuffled, n, selectSeed(scr, task))

  lockAll(cases, order, n)
  let fired = pollOnce(cases, shuffled, n, s)
  if (fired < n) {
    unlockAll(cases, order, n)
    return fired
  }
  if (hasDefault) {
    unlockAll(cases, order, n)
    return n
  }

  // Nothing ready and no default: register on every case, then park. As in the
  // plain send/recv paths, registration happens under the SAME lock hold that
  // observed nothing ready, so there is no gap for a waker to slip through and
  // no recheck to perform.
  *(scr + scrSelDone) = 0
  *(scr + scrSelWinner) = 0
  let sel = int(scr + scrSelDone)

  let j = 0
  while (j < n) {
    let c = cases + j * selCaseWords
    let ch = taskAt(*(c + scChan))
    let w = nodeAt(scr, j)
    *(w + wTask) = task
    *(w + wElem) = int(c + scWord)
    *(w + wOk) = int(c + scOk)
    *(w + wSelect) = sel
    *(w + wCase) = j
    if (*(c + scDir) == chanDirRecv) {
      wqPush(ch, chRecvHead, w)
    } else {
      wqPush(ch, chSendHead, w)
    }
    j = j + 1
  }
  unlockAll(cases, order, n)

  schedPark()

  // Woken by exactly one case. Every OTHER registration must come off its queue
  // before this returns: the waiter nodes live in this task's scratch, and a
  // stale queue entry pointing at one would be handed a value destined for a
  // select that is no longer waiting. `chan.zig` unlinks here for the same
  // reason — its nodes are on the returning stack frame.
  //
  // A case that was never registered is a harmless no-op, which is what makes
  // `wqRemove` tolerant of an absent target.
  let winner = *(scr + scrSelWinner)
  lockAll(cases, order, n)
  let m = 0
  while (m < n) {
    if (m != winner) {
      let c = cases + m * selCaseWords
      let ch = taskAt(*(c + scChan))
      let w = nodeAt(scr, m)
      if (*(c + scDir) == chanDirRecv) {
        wqRemove(ch, chRecvHead, int(w))
      } else {
        wqRemove(ch, chSendHead, int(w))
      }
    }
    m = m + 1
  }
  unlockAll(cases, order, n)
  return winner
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GC root scanning (ABI.md §11)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A buffered word is reachable ONLY through the ring buffer while it sits
// unreceived — no stack and no register holds it — so the collector must treat
// every `isRef` channel's buffered words as roots or sweep live values.
//
// `chan.zig` exposes this as `scanRegistryRoots(mark)` and `forEachBuffered
// (visit)`, both taking a function pointer. `@nosplit` rejects a call through a
// function value (E0075), so the traversal is inverted into a CURSOR the
// collector drives — the same change `runtime/sched` made to
// `forEachOtherStack` for the same reason, and the same one this file made to
// the vtable.
//
// The registry itself is a caller-owned two-word block, so this module keeps no
// mutable module state: registered channels are process-lifetime (v1 has no
// channel-free primitive, ABI.md §11), so nothing is ever unlinked and a plain
// head-insertion under a lock is the whole structure.
//
//   0  head channel address
//   1  lock — an i32 cell
export const chanRegWords: int = 2
const crHead: int = 0
const crLock: int = 1

export @nosplit function chanRegInit(reg: *i64) {
  *(reg + crHead) = 0
  atomicStore(spinCellAt(reg, crLock), 0)
}

// Opt a channel into root scanning. `chan.zig`'s `register`, called only by the
// ABI's channel constructor and only for `is_ref` channels.
export @nosplit function chanRegister(reg: *i64, ch: *i64) {
  spinAcquire(spinCellAt(reg, crLock))
  *(ch + chRegNext) = *(reg + crHead)
  *(reg + crHead) = int(ch)
  spinRelease(spinCellAt(reg, crLock))
}

export @nosplit function chanRegHead(reg: *i64): int {
  let h = *(reg + crHead)
  return h
}

export @nosplit function chanRegNext(ch: *i64): int {
  let nx = *(ch + chRegNext)
  return nx
}

// Whether this channel's buffered words are GC references.
export @nosplit function chanIsRef(ch: *i64): bool {
  return *(ch + chIsRef) != 0
}

// The `i`th currently-buffered word, in queue order. The collector walks
// `0 .. chanCount(ch)` under the channel's lock, which it takes with
// `chanLockCell` — `chan.zig`'s `forEachBuffered` took the lock itself around
// the whole visit, and a cursor cannot, so the lock is handed out instead of
// being taken and released per element.
export @nosplit function chanBufferedAt(ch: *i64, i: int): int {
  let buf = taskAt(*(ch + chBuf))
  let v = *(buf + (*(ch + chHead) + i) % *(ch + chCap))
  return v
}

// This channel's lock cell, for a collector that must hold it across a walk.
export @nosplit function chanLockCell(ch: *i64): *i32 {
  return spinCellAt(ch, chLock)
}
